<div class="article-body">
    <div class="callout">
      <h2 id="glance" style="margin-top:0">At a glance</h2>
      <ol class="stop-steps">
        <li><strong>Decompress?</strong> You hang from your arms, so the spine carries less compressive load while you hang. No study has measured disc height or pressure during a bodyweight hang.</li>
        <li><strong>Back pain:</strong> a Cochrane review (Wegner 2013) found traction gives little or no benefit for low back pain.</li>
        <li><strong>What people report:</strong> some online accounts describe relief and others describe pain. Neither is trial evidence.</li>
        <li><strong>Stop and get advice</strong> for sharp or radiating pain, leg weakness, numbness or faintness, and before hanging with a diagnosed disc problem.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Common practice is short hangs on 3 non-consecutive days a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
