    <Fragment set:html={`<h2>Does a dead hang decompress the spine?</h2>
        <p>A dead hang takes your body weight through your arms, so the spine carries less compressive load while you hang. No study has measured disc height or disc pressure during a bodyweight hang, and none shows a lasting change. "Decompression" is a popular label, not a tested result.</p>
        <p>Clinicians use the word traction for pulling force along the spine. A dead hang is a form of traction that is uncontrolled and uses your own body weight.</p>

        <h2>What does the research on spinal traction show?</h2>
        <p>A Cochrane review of traction for low back pain (Wegner et al. 2013) found little or no effect on pain, function or return to work. Clinical traction applies a controlled force under supervision. A dead hang uses your own body weight, so those results do not transfer directly. No study shows a lasting effect from dead hangs on the spine.</p>

        <h2>Can dead hangs help lower back stiffness?</h2>
        <p>Some people find short hangs ease lower back stiffness, but relief is not guaranteed and no trial has tested it. Common practice is 15 to 30 second hangs, 3 to 4 sets, on 3 non-consecutive days a week. This is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>
        <p>Most adults have low back pain at some point. Estimates vary with how it is defined.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Common Hang Routine for Back Stiffness</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Hang for 15 to 30 seconds per set.</li>
            <li>Rest 60 seconds between sets.</li>
            <li>Complete 3 to 4 sets per session.</li>
            <li>Start with 3 sessions a week on non-consecutive days.</li>
            <li>Stop if symptoms worsen. See a clinician if stiffness has not eased.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>This back stiffness routine is common training practice, not a tested prescription. Comfortable short hangs are easier to repeat than long ones.</p>

        <h2>Can dead hangs help sciatica or a herniated disc?</h2>
        <p>No trial has tested dead hangs for sciatica or a herniated disc. Online accounts range from quick relief to flared pain, and neither counts as evidence. Ask a physician or physiotherapist before hanging with a diagnosed herniation.</p>
        <p>Disc herniations range from mild bulges to severe extrusions that compress nerve roots. No evidence shows that dead hangs help a herniated disc. Some people report that hanging aggravates their disc problem.</p>
        <p>Severe herniations with significant nerve compression are a contraindication for dead hangs. Symptoms like leg weakness, numbness or loss of bladder control need immediate medical attention.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Important Medical Notice</h3>
          <p>Ask a physician or physiotherapist before using dead hangs with a diagnosed herniated disc. Imaging results help a clinician judge whether traction is appropriate for your condition. Self-treating a severe herniation may worsen nerve compression.</p>
        </div>

        <p>If your clinician approves hanging then keep your feet on the ground so the bar carries only part of your weight. Monitor symptoms during and after each session. Any increase in radiating leg pain means you should stop and reassess with your provider.</p>

        <h2>What do people report about dead hangs and back pain?</h2>
        <p>Forum accounts of dead hangs and back pain point in both directions. They are individual stories and not evidence of what hanging does. The table sets common reports beside what is actually known.</p>
        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Reports about dead hangs and back pain compared with evidence" tabindex="0"><table>
          <caption>What people report and what is known</caption>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">What people report</th><th scope="col">What is known</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Relief after a few hangs, including sciatica-type pain</td><td>Individual accounts. No trial of dead hangs for back pain or sciatica.</td><td>Treat it as one person's experience, not a treatment.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Pain, tingling or a pull in the back after hanging</td><td>Also reported, and not predicted by any study.</td><td>Stop if pain is sharp, radiating or lasts after the session.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Back feels like it needs to pop, or pops</td><td>A description cannot show the cause.</td><td>See a clinician if it hurts.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Feeling faint while hanging</td><td>Reported in forum posts. The cause cannot be established from them.</td><td>Lower yourself, and see a doctor if it recurs.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Traction eases back pain</td><td>A Cochrane review found little or no benefit for low back pain.</td><td>Do not rely on hanging as treatment.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <h2>How long should you dead hang for back stiffness?</h2>
        <p>Short, comfortable hangs are easier to repeat than long ones. The table shows example dosage by level. It is common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table>
          <caption>Example hang dosage by level (practical guidance, not trial-tested)</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Level</th>
              <th scope="col">Duration Per Set</th>
              <th scope="col">Sets</th>
              <th scope="col">Frequency</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Beginner</td>
              <td>10 to 15 seconds</td>
              <td>3</td>
              <td>3 days a week, non-consecutive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Intermediate</td>
              <td>20 to 30 seconds</td>
              <td>3 to 4</td>
              <td>3 days a week, non-consecutive</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Advanced</td>
              <td>30 to 60 seconds</td>
              <td>3 to 4</td>
              <td>3 days a week, more only if pain free</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <p>Accumulating 60 to 90 seconds of total hang time per session is a common target. Split it into sets that match your grip capacity. Grip strength often limits hang duration first. A <a href="/doorway-bars/">doorway pull-up bar</a> at home makes it easy to fit a hang into the day.</p>

        <h2>Which stretches do people pair with dead hangs?</h2>
        <p>Many people pair dead hangs with stretches for the hips and lower back, such as cat-cow, child's pose, a hip flexor stretch and a knee-to-chest stretch. These are common practice and are not tested as a treatment for back pain.</p>

        <h3>Cat-Cow</h3>
        <p>Start on hands and knees. Arch your back upward like an angry cat. Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Drop your belly toward the floor and lift your head. Hold 2 to 3 seconds. Alternate for 10 to 15 repetitions. Cat-cow is a common gentle mobility drill.</p>

        <h3>Child's Pose</h3>
        <p>Kneel and sit your hips back toward your heels. Extend your arms forward along the floor. Hold for 30 to 60 seconds. Many people do child's pose right after their hang sets.</p>

        <h3>Hip Flexor Stretch</h3>
        <p>Some people feel tightness at the front of the hip. Kneel on one knee with the other foot forward. Push your hips forward until you feel a stretch in the front of the rear hip. Hold 30 to 45 seconds per side.</p>

        <h3>Knee-to-Chest Stretch</h3>
        <p>Lie on your back. Pull one knee toward your chest with both hands. Hold 20 to 30 seconds per side. Keep the movement gentle and stop if it hurts.</p>

        <h2>When should you avoid dead hangs with a back problem?</h2>
        <p>Avoid dead hangs during acute back pain, with a severe herniation, with spinal stenosis unless a clinician agrees, and after spinal surgery until your surgeon clears you. Hanging may make an existing problem worse, and symptoms such as saddle numbness need urgent care.</p>

        <h3>Acute Injury</h3>
        <p>Do not hang during a flare-up of acute back pain. Wait for it to settle and ask a clinician if unsure.</p>

        <h3>Severe Herniation</h3>
        <p>Large herniations that compress the spinal cord or cause significant nerve deficits are not candidates for self-directed traction. Bilateral leg weakness, saddle numbness or bladder dysfunction require urgent medical evaluation. Dead hangs cannot address these conditions.</p>

        <h3>Spinal Stenosis</h3>
        <p>Spinal stenosis narrows the spinal canal through bony changes. Stenosis symptoms often ease in forward bending and worsen in backward bending. Ask your clinician whether hanging suits your stenosis.</p>

        <h3>Post-Surgical</h3>
        <p>Ask your surgeon whether hanging is allowed. Follow your surgeon's timeline for returning to hanging.</p>

        <p>The <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> covers more precautions for people with existing conditions.</p>

        <h2>How do you start dead hangs safely with a stiff back?</h2>
        <p>Many people build up in stages, starting with feet on the ground, instead of going straight to a full hang. The four stages below are common training practice, not a tested prescription. Stop at any sign of sharp pain.</p>

        <div class="callout">
          <h3>Gradual Progression for Back Stiffness</h3>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Stage 1:</strong> Stand under the bar and grip it with both feet flat on the ground. Bend your knees slightly so your arms carry part of your bodyweight. Hold 15 to 20 seconds for 3 sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 2:</strong> Lift one foot off the ground at a time and alternate feet between sets. Hold 15 to 20 seconds for 3 sets.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 3:</strong> Lift both feet off the ground for a full dead hang. Start with 10 to 15 second holds and add time as your grip allows.</li>
            <li><strong>Stage 4:</strong> Maintain 3 to 4 sets of 20 to 30 seconds in 3 sessions a week.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <p>Stop at any sign of sharp pain. Some dull aching or mild discomfort can occur while adapting. Sharp, shooting or radiating pain means the load is too high. Reduce the weight your arms carry or the duration and try again.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/how-to-deadhang/">dead hang form guide</a> covers grip placement and breathing.</p>

